  "content_list": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "At the start of the decade, <i>British New Wave</i> director <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 32765, 'screen_type': person}\">Tony Richardson</a> (1928-1991) swapped social realism for farcical comedy in <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 5769, 'screen_type': movie}\">Tom Jones</a> (1963), an adaptation of <i>Henry Fielding</i>'s 18th-century novel. Provocative and daring, it follows the sexual adventures of young Tom, played by an <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 3926, 'screen_type': person}\">Albert Finney</a> (1936-2019) completely at ease in the role."
    },
    {
      "type": "video",
      "video_id": "ahFARm2j38c",
      "information": {
        "contentTitle": "Final scene from The Graduate",
        "contentText": "The film became known, among other reasons, for its soundtrack by folk duo <i>Simon & Garfunkel</i> (featuring songs that would become pop hits, such as <i>The Sound of Silence</i> and <i>Mrs. Robinson</i>) and for launching the career of the then-unknown Dustin Hoffman. In addition, some of its scenes became archetypes for many films of a similar genre produced to this day, and have even been parodied many times across various media.",
        "source": "YouTube: <i>cinefilikos</i> channel"
      }
    },
