    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "To 21st-century audiences, <i>Tom Jones</i> is a prudish account of Tom's amorous encounters, but at the time the film was considered licentious. When Jones and Mrs. Waters dine together, Richardson allusively depicts the couple's lustful desires as they suck oysters, tear into chicken legs, and fill their mouths with juicy pears."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "Cinema's reexamination of social mores was not limited to Western society. In Japan, directors such as <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 20025, 'screen_type': person}\">Shohei Imamura</a> (1926-2006) showed women's new role and uninhibited sexuality in films such as <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 42984, 'screen_type': movie}\">Nippon konchûki</a> (The Insect Woman, 1963), which tells the story of a young peasant woman turned <i>geisha</i>, raped by her stepfather and disgraced by her \"<i>carnal desire</i>\". The film traces Japan's history before and after the wars of the 20th century, as tradition gives way to a liberal industrial society."
    },
    {
      "type": "video",
      "video_id": "e83L05ptpAE",
      "information": {
        "contentTitle": "Scene from I Am Curious (Yellow)",
        "contentText": "I Am Curious (Yellow) documented the sexual revolution in Swedish society through the experiences of a rebellious young woman. It contains scenes of nudity and depictions of sexual intercourse.",
        "source": "YouTube: <i>Ismail</i> channel"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "Over the course of the decade, cinema became more open to forbidden subjects and more inclined to reflect what was happening in an increasingly liberal society. The success of <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 1052, 'screen_type': movie}\">Blow-Up</a> (1966), directed by <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 15189, 'screen_type': person}\">Michelangelo Antonioni</a> (1912-2007), was due in part to its explicit sex scenes and its treatment of drug abuse in contemporary <i>fashion</i>-world London."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "<a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 649, 'screen_type': movie}\">Belle de Jour</a> (1967) shocked audiences with the story of a Parisian housewife who works as a prostitute during the day to fulfill her sadomasochistic fantasies. <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 133471, 'screen_type': person}\">Vilgot Sjöman</a> (1924-2006) caused outrage with his frank treatment of sex and violence in <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 42695, 'screen_type': movie}\">Jag är nyfiken - en film i gult</a> (I Am Curious (Yellow), 1967), the story of a promiscuous young woman."
    },
    {
      "type": "video",
      "video_id": "_zeza1xeWKM",
      "information": {
        "contentTitle": "Scene from Blow-Up",
        "contentText": "The film, which won the Grand Prix at the Cannes Film Festival, was written by Antonioni and Tonino Guerra, and its cast includes <i>David Hemmings, Vanessa Redgrave, Sarah Miles, Jane Birkin</i> - in the first full-frontal nude scenes in a mainstream British film - and supermodel <i>Veruschka</i>, who plays herself and has a scene that the film magazine Premiere once called \"<i>the sexiest moment in movie history</i>\".",
        "source": "YouTube: <i>Movieclips</i> channel"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "<a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 5342, 'screen_type': person}\">Mike Nichols</a> (b. 1931) perfectly captured the ebb and flow of American society on the brink of the sexual revolution in <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 37247, 'screen_type': movie}\">The Graduate</a> (1967), a study of sex across generations. If most American cinema had been conventional up to that point, Nichols's film bridges the old world and the new, pointing toward the more experimental cinema that would arrive in the 1970s."
    },
    {
      "type": "block_special",
      "title": "Soundtracks and Cinema",
      "image": {
        "image_type": "local",
        "url": "img_mrs_robinson",
        "style": {
          "scale_type": "center_crop"
        }
      },
      "description": "After asking Paul Simon to produce material for <i>The Graduate</i>, Mike Nichols decided to use songs from two earlier <i>Simon & Garfunkel</i> albums, along with part of the then-unfinished \"<i>Mrs. Robinson</i>\", in addition to music by <i>Dave Grusin</i>. The film, and cinema in general, profited from the use of popular material. The soundtrack was essential to the film's success, and the album reached the top of the charts before being replaced by <i>Bookends</i>, the one containing the full version of \"<i>Mrs. Robinson</i>\" and other songs written but not used in the film. The way the music was used is also striking. \"<i>The Sounds (later 'Sound') of Silence</i>\" plays at the beginning, at the end, and during the sequence of Benjamin at home intercut with his affair with Mrs. Robinson. The lyrics, from \"<i>Hello darkness, my old friend</i>\", suggest Benjamin's innermost thoughts while never connecting directly to the narrative, and the bleak lines are offset by the upbeat vocals."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "As the 1960s drew to a close and censorship laws were relaxed, films began to shock for shock's sake. In the <i>kitsch</i> science fiction film <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 8069, 'screen_type': movie}\">Barbarella</a> (1968), <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 6352, 'screen_type': person}\">Jane Fonda</a> (b. 1937) plays a 20th-century astronaut who travels to the planet <i>Tau Ceti</i> in search of the missing scientist Durand-Durand."
    },
    {
      "type": "video",
      "video_id": "NX2hTObHfxM",
      "information": {
        "contentTitle": "Scene from Barbarella ",
        "contentText": "Shot at producer <i>Dino De Laurentiis</i>'s studios in Rome, simultaneously in French and English (the international version), with most of the actors playing their roles in both languages. Its chaotic, ahead-of-its-time screenplay, however, led to its failure at the box office and with audiences upon release - despite a big advertising campaign featuring Jane Fonda, who even posed in costume for the men's magazine Penthouse. Over time, the film and its soundtrack became a cultural reference, influencing pop culture.",
        "source": "YouTube: <i>Flickfeast</i> channel"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "With <i>Barbarella</i>'s constant and inexplicable changes of see-through outfits, its hints of lesbianism in the character's encounters with the lascivious Great Tyrant (Anita Pallenberg), and its scenes with sex devices, this extravagant cult film has an unquestionably sadomasochistic bent. At the end of the decade, <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 2778, 'screen_type': person}\">Dennis Hopper</a> (1936-2010) shared the screenplay and the lead role of the <i>road movie Easy Rider</i> (1969) with <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 8949, 'screen_type': person}\">Peter Fonda</a> (1940-2019)."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_text": "They play hippies on a motorcycle trip from Los Angeles to New Orleans. Their drug-fueled flings with naked women start out innocent and end up frightening, but are never explicit. Hopper used the new freedom available to filmmakers to pose disturbing questions to the nation's youth. The revolution was no longer sexual, but one of ideas.",
      "content_credits": "Summary based on the text by <i>Carol King</i>, published in the book \"<i>Cinema: The Whole Story</i>\" by <i>Philip Kemp</i>."
    },
